## What are Closures?

Closures are a fundamental JavaScript concept where inner functions maintain access to variables from their outer (lexical) scope, even after the outer function completes execution.

## React Use Case

```javascript
function Counter({ initialCount }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(initialCount);

  // This callback creates a closure over 'count'
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(count + 1); // Accesses outer 'count' variable
  }, [count]);

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Count: {count}</button>;
}
```

## Key Benefits

- Preserves access to outer scope variables
- Enables powerful patterns in React development
- Maintains state consistency across component re-renders
- Supports memoization and performance optimization

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to compose DOM, network, and offline features with beginner-friendly depth. It exercises fetch error handling, WebSocket lifecycle with backoff, and Service Worker cache strategies in a single cohesive scenario.

## Key Concepts
- fetch for initial data and error handling
- WebSocket lifecycle with exponential backoff
- deduplication and minimal DOM updates
- Service Worker caching of assets and payloads for offline mode

## Code Example
```javascript
// Fetch wrapper
async function fetchFeed(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Network error');
  return res.json();
}

// WebSocket manager with backoff
function createFeedWS(url, onUpdate) {
  let ws = new WebSocket(url);
  let retry = 0;
  ws.onmessage = (ev) => {
    const item = JSON.parse(ev.data);
    onUpdate(item);
  };
  ws.onclose = () => {
    const delay = Math.min(30000, 1000 * Math.pow(2, retry++));
    setTimeout(() => {
      ws = createFeedWS(url, onUpdate);
    }, delay);
  };
  return ws;
}

// Simple feed renderer with dedupe
const seen = new Set();
function handleUpdate(item) {
  if (seen.has(item.id)) return;
  seen.add(item.id);
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = item.text;
  feedList.prepend(li); // minimal DOM update
}

